==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|raptor||robber|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|rapere||seize|lang=la}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɹæpt/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"r{pt/}}
*: {{rhymes|æpt}}
* Homophones: [[rapped]], [[wrapped]], [[wrapt]]

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{uncomparable|archaic}} Snatched, taken away; abducted.
# {{uncomparable}} Lifted up into the air; transported into heaven.
# {{comparable}} Very [[interested]], [[involved]] in something, [[absorbed]], [[transfixed]]; [[fascinated]] or [[engrossed]].
#: ''The children watched in '''rapt''' attention as the magician produced object after object from his hat.''
#* '''1851-2''', [[w:George W. M. Reynolds|George W. M. Reynolds]], ''The Necromancer'', in ''Reynolds′s Miscellany'', republished 1857; 2008, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=DO1PXInE7SoC&pg=PA247&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=XJX6T6bVH6-XiAeDodTaBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more|most%20rapt%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 247],
#*: It was an enthusiasm of the most '''rapt''' and holy kind.
#* '''1906''', [[w:Ford Madox Ford|Ford Madox Ford]], ''[[w:The Fifth Queen|The Fifth Queen; And How She Came to Court]]'', ''Works of Ford Madox Ford'', 2011, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=jlHaMJjcLgEC&pg=PT144&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=P9z6T7HgE8SpiAeV94HQBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more|most%20rapt%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false unnumbered page],
#*: Her expression grew more '''rapt'''; she paused as if she had lost the thread of the words and then spoke again, gazing far out over the hall as jugglers do in performing feats of balancing:{{...}}.
#* '''1908''', [[w:Kenneth Grahame|Kenneth Grahame]], ''[[w:The Wind in the Willows|The Wind in the Willows]]''
#*: The Rat never answered, if indeed he heard. '''Rapt''', transported, trembling, he was possessed in all his senses by this new divine thing that caught up his helpless soul and swung and [[dandle|dandled]] it, a powerless but happy infant in a strong [[sustaining]] grasp.
#* '''1998''', Derel Leebaert, ''Present at the Creation'', Derek Leebaert (editor), ''The Future of the Electronic Marketplace'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=yo43oViNGMgC&pg=PA24&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=XJX6T6bVH6-XiAeDodTaBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more|most%20rapt%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 24],
# {{comparable}} [[enthusiastic|Enthusiatic]]; [[ecstatic]], [[elated]], [[happy]].
#: ''He was '''rapt''' with his exam results.''
#* '''1996''', James Richard Giles, Wanda H. Giles, ''American Novelists Since World War II: Fifth Series'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=58oUAQAAIAAJ&q=%22more|most+rapt%22+australia+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+australia+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=OeD6T9LoFsaZiQf_wYT-Bg&redir_esc=y page 139],
#*: Creatures who navigate long-distance migrations — including the green turtles, wind birds, or great cranes — draw his most '''rapt''' commentaries.
#* '''2010''', Michael Reichert, Richard Hawley, ''Reaching Boys, Teaching Boys: Strategies that Work—and Why'', John Wiley & Sons, US, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=MXI9mYLmWcQC&pg=PA119&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+australia+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=OeD6T9LoFsaZiQf_wYT-Bg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more|most%20rapt%22%20australia%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 121],
#*: Even in the most '''rapt''' accounts of independent student work, there appears an appreciative acknowledgment of the teacher′s having determined just the right amount of room necessary to build autonomy without risking frustration and failure.
#* '''2010''', [[w:Caroline Overington|Caroline Overington]], ''I Came to Say Goodbye'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=2E8pq6yTCRsC&pg=PA201&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+australia+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=OeD6T9LoFsaZiQf_wYT-Bg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more|most%20rapt%22%20australia%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 201],
#*: One bloke I met in the pub was the owner of the local meatworks. He was '''rapt''' to have the Sudanese, and if 1600 more were coming – that was the rumour – well, he′d have been even more '''rapt'''.
#* '''2012''', Greig Caigou, ''Wild Horizons: More Great Hunting Adventures'', HarperCollins (New Zealand), [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=TlDvzh-FtEEC&pg=PT251&dq=%22more|most+rapt%22+australia+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=OeD6T9LoFsaZiQf_wYT-Bg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q&f=false unnumbered page],
#*: These are worthy aspects of the hunt to give some consideration to with the next generation, because market forces want us to get more '''rapt''' with ever more sophisticated gear and an algorithmic conquering of animal instinct.

